    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A study was made of the mathematical theory of gas well build-up and draw-down testing for the purpose of generalizing and extending the real gas pseudo-pressure concept. This research considered the following effects on gas well testing: (1) variation of real gas properties with pressure, (2) formation damage around the wellbore, (3) wellbore storage, (4) turbulent, or non-Darcy, flow near the wellbore, and (5) a vertical fracture at the wellbore.The two -dimensional mathematical model for the case of a vertical fracture at the wellbore was solved by numerical computer methods. The Alternating Direction Implicit Procedure (ADIP) was used to solve the finite-difference equations. For cases which did not involve the vertical fracture at the wellbore, radial symmetry was assumed and a simpler one-dimensional mathematical model was solved by computer methods.Analysis of numerical results from the computer models showed that the real gas pseudo-pressure solutions for the constant-rate draw-down case are generally very similar to the solutions for liquid flow. The formation flow capacity and the skin effect due to formation damage usually can be determined accurately by methods analogous to those used in liquid well testing theory. However, when turbulence is significant, the formation flow capacity calculated from the constant-rate draw-down test can be lower than the true formation flow capacity. A numerical case was run in which this error was 35%.For build-up testing, the real gas pseudo-pressure solutions are also similar to the solutions for liquid flow. Wellbore damage, wellbore storage, and turbulence are all short-time effects which do not directly affect the calculation of formation flow capacity from the build-up plot. However, when the production rate is extremely high, during the flow period, the slope of the build-up plot can be affected and the calculated formation flow capacity can be slightly low.Gas well testing in a vertically fractured well results in an apparent negative skin affect, similar to the liquid flow case. If a straight-line portion occurs on the draw-down curve before exterior boundary effects influence the wellbore pressure, the formation flow capacity can be calculated accurately from the slope. An alternate method has been proposed for calculating formation flow capacity from very early transient data. In principle, this proposed method can be used, under ideal conditions, when the straight-line portion of the draw-down curve is obscured by exterior boundary effects.It has been shown that the formation flow capacity can be determined directly from the slope of either a pressure or pressure-squared build-up or draw-down p l o t .By this method, the slope is taken at a point and converted to the equivalent real gas pseudo-pressure slope by using the gas properties at that point. This method of calculating formation flow capacity is more general than other methods which use either initial gas properties or average gas properties.",
